        "custom_liquid": "<script type=\"application/ld+json\">\n{\n  \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n  \"@graph\": [\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"BlogPosting\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/pennsylvania-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#article\",\n  \t\"isPartOf\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/pennsylvania-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#webpage\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"mainEntityOfPage\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/pennsylvania-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#webpage\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"headline\": \"Pennsylvania Emergency Vehicle Light Laws: Statute, Flash Rate Limits and Equipment Standards\",\n  \t\"description\": \"75 Pa.C.S. 4571 and 4572 plus the 67 Pa. Code Ch. 173 specs: flash rate limits, SAE color standards and the $500 penalty. Reviewed August 2026.\",\n  \t\"abstract\": \"Pennsylvania regulates emergency lighting in two layers. 75 Pa.C.S. §§ 4571 and 4572 assign color by vehicle class, and 67 Pa. Code Chapter 173 sets the binding equipment specification. Pennsylvania requires a flash rate between 60 and 260 flashes per minute when observed from a fixed position, under 67 Pa. Code § 173.3. Flashing and revolving lights in Pennsylvania must meet or exceed the Chromaticity Coordinates of the CIE 1931 Standard Colorimetric System as provided in SAE Standard J578d or a subsequent SAE standard. White, clear, red, blue, amber and yellow are the only colors permitted for flashing or revolving lights in Pennsylvania under 67 Pa. Code § 173.3(a). Combination red and blue lights in Pennsylvania may be used only on police, sheriff, coroner, medical examiner or fire police vehicles, under 75 Pa.C.S. § 4571(b). Unauthorized use of emergency warning equipment in Pennsylvania is a summary offense punishable by a fine of not less than $500 and not more than $1,000 under 75 Pa.C.S. § 4571(e).\",\n  \t\"speakable\": {\n    \t\"@type\": \"SpeakableSpecification\",\n    \t\"cssSelector\": [\n  